```text
Discussion followed on previous amendments and costs associated with implementing the recycling
program.  Mr. Collins moved to amend the fee for 60 gal. containers from $2.00 to $3.00, seconded by
Dr. Rinne. Upon inquiry, Public Works Director Jim Elias clarified staff’s initial fee proposal for 60
gal. containers was $5.00 and that a $3.00 fee was acceptable as a current option as data is still being
compiled as to operational costs for the program.  Upon inquiry pursuant to a point of clarification
being raised, it was advised the fee schedule would also be amended in conjunction with the ordinance.
Comments followed on an amendment approved at the last City Council meeting vs. the proposed
amendment.  Mr. Elias noted the proposed amendment would also affect Note 8 included in the
ordinance relative to the 60 gallon trash/60 gallon recycle fee (ie: $15 to $16).  Mr. Collins, with
concurrence by Dr. Rinne as second to the motion, revised his previous amendment to include the
change in fee also be reflected in Note 8 for 60 gal trash/60 gal recycle from $15 to $16.  Comments
followed regarding discussion held at the Finance Committee meeting regarding costs and proposed
fees.  Mayor Kaysen advised studies are currently being conducted to collect data to present a true cost
of services that will be reviewed in conjunction with the next regularly scheduled rate review ordinance
which is scheduled for an effective date of July 1, 2010.  Motion to amend carried.  Voting “yes” – all
members of the governing body with the exception of Ms. Broyles, Ms. Case and Mr. Pierson voting
“no”. Comments followed in support of sustaining the residential recycling program and recouping
costs already invested. Main motion as amended carried.  Voting “yes” – all members of the governing
body with the exception of Ms. Case voting “no.” (#3879)
